# Codice Perfetto for laravel-oidc

The accepted Signaculum SSO design (2026-11-05) and the "Protocol profile" section of the README are the source of truth. This rule does not add product scope.

## Ponytail

Pick the simplest solution that works. Stop at the first rung that holds: YAGNI, standard library, platform, installed dependency, one line, then the minimum code.

- No unrequested abstractions, dependencies, or boilerplate.
- Deletion over addition. Boring over clever. Fewest files possible.
- Mark an intentional shortcut with a `ponytail:` comment that names the ceiling and the upgrade path.
- Never lazy about trust-boundary validation, token validation, SSRF, or anything security-relevant. This package is an authentication boundary.
- Non-trivial logic leaves one runnable check behind.

## Generic, not Signaculum

- The package must work with any standard OIDC provider: Keycloak, Zitadel, Authentik, Entra ID, Google, Signaculum.
- Anything provider-specific (the `orgs` claim, the `qiick.orgs` scope, non-standard `amr` values) goes behind config or a mapping. Never hard-code it.

## Names

- Full words. No `cfg`, `res`, `req`, `val`, `ctx`, or `idx`.
- Booleans and boolean methods use `is`, `has`, `can`, or `should`.
- Role suffixes match the architecture test (`tests/Arch/NamingArchitectureTest.php`).

## Documentation

- `declare(strict_types=1)` in every PHP file.
- English PHPDoc on classes and non-trivial methods: why, array shapes, side effects, and `@throws`. Cite the spec section (OIDC Core 3.1.3.7, RFC 7636, ...) when a check exists because a spec requires it.
- Explicit curly braces and a blank line around control-flow blocks.
- Do not call `dd`, `dump`, `var_dump`, or `env()` outside `config/`.
